  "strategy": "1. Approach and Rationale\n   Central Idea: We will explore a novel connection between the minimum overlap problem and properties of balanced incomplete block designs (BIBDs) and low-discrepancy point sets. These combinatorial structures naturally minimize overlaps in periodic constructions. Our method duality between minimizing the correlation between a set with its shifts, I(k) = \u222bh(x)(1-h(x+k))dx, aligns perfectly with the autocorrelation properties of BIBDs.\n\n   Rationale: If we view h(x) as a characteristic function of a periodic point set in [0,2], the minimization problem becomes finding the periodic structure with lowest maximum autocorrelation. Conventional local optimization is insufficient; we need global exploration of combinatorial structures. Recent breakthroughs in constructing Steinermallow designs show direct connections to minimal correlations, suggesting this path offers unexplored potential.\n\n   Limitations: Requires exponential time for exhaustive search, but uses optimization to reduce dimensionality. The valid fallback ensures we still return progress.\n\n2. Mathematical Specification\n   - Representation: h(x) defined via a periodic point set S in [0,2] with |S|=n/2 points\n   - Objective: C5 = max_k{ \u222b_0^{2-k} h(x)(1-h(x{k}))dx }\n   - Constraints: \u2211_{i=1}^{n} h[i] = n/2, 0\u2264h[i]\u22641\n   - Variables: Period length \u03c4, number of distinct phases m, and generation vector g \u2208 \u211d^\u03c4\n\n   The key formulation involves viewing h(x) as:\n   h(x) = (1/\u03c4) \u2211_{j=1}^m \u03b4(x - (x_j + integer*\u03c4)) \n\n   With E[x_j - x_k] defined by autocorrelation stability (van der Corput sequence properties)\n\n3. Implementation Procedure\n   - Input validation if initial_h_values provided and n_points\u22641000\n   - Optimize period lengths and phases using genetic algorithm (GA) with tournament selection\n   - Use withering rule for efficient elimination of suboptimal candidates\n   - Repair infeasible solutions via minimal weight insertion/deletion\n   - Termination: 30 minutes execution time or convergence\n   - Submit construction: optimized point set as step function\n\n   Pseudocode:\n   ```\n   def recursive_search(period, population_size, depth):\n     if depth ==0: evaluate candidate period via correlation\n     else: branch on period structure, use tournament selection\n   ```\n\n4. Search Choices and Variations\n   - Default period range: [1.0, 4.0] with base resolution 100\n   - Population size: 50-2000 (increases with problem size)\n   - Mutation: phase perturbation in line with discrepancy measure\n   - Maintain diversity: minimum population size reset every 50th generation\n   - Deflation approach: use lower discrepancy generator to start branches\n\n5. Computational Budget and Robustness\n   - Dominant cost: exponential in period complexity\n   - Budget allocation: 30% initial random, 40% GA pruning, 30% final output verification\n   - Check remaining time before each GA iteration\n   - Maintain current best candidate via generational stamp\n   - Fallback: construct uniform design if full time used\n\n6. Validation and Return Contract\n   - Feasibility check: ensure \u2211h=125\u00b1\u03b5, 0\u2264h\u22641\n   - Objective recomputation with finite differences for numerical stability\n   - Sanction mismatched dimensions: throw DimensionError upon return\n   - Validation checks:\n     1. \u2211_i h[i]*dx\u22481.0\n     2. \u2200i: 0\u2264h[i]\u22641\n     3. np.alltrue(0s and 1s uniformly distributed)",
